---
Method: worked in a `cp -a` backup + a separate scratch copy of
.worktrees/spending-plan-restore (base 950e2d9). All new probe test files
lived only in the scratch copy; the real worktree was diffed byte-identical
against the backup before and after (`diff -rq` exit 0). No write/mutation
touched the worktree itself. Ran `go build ./...`, `go vet ./...`, and
`go test -count=1 ./internal/handlers/whatif/ ./internal/models/
./internal/templates/...` directly in the real worktree (read-only) — all
green, matching the manifest.

Attacks tried, per the brief:

1. Hash-guard sensitivity to SP1's own field. Wrote a probe
   (TestProbe_AppliedEvidenceStaleAfterSpendingSearchEdit) that edits ONLY
   `SpendingSearch.MaxShortfallPct` after a real apply (not DiscountRate,
   which is all the shipped test touches) and re-saves. `appliedSpendingEvidenceFresh`
   correctly went false and `POST /whatif/spending/applied/graph` returned
   409. PASS — the shipped test's narrower fixture (DiscountRate only) did
   not miss a real gap.

2. Hash collision across two independent applies. Probe
   (TestProbe_DifferentAppliesProduceDifferentHashes) confirmed distinct,
   non-empty hashes; inspected the marshaled evidence directly (int64 seeds
   via `,string` tag round-trip correctly including a negative selection
   seed).

3. Re-apply (second Apply on the same scenario) fully replaces evidence —
   this is the brief's explicit "SpendingSearch changing on a LATER Apply"
   surface, read as "does the OLD evidence survive/leak". Wrote
   TestProbe_ReApplyReplacesEvidenceAndHashVerifies: applied option A
   (seed 111111), then option B (seed 222222) on the same settings file.
   The persisted evidence after the second apply carries B's seed, not A's,
   and its hash verifies against the current settings. No stale evidence
   survives a re-apply.

4. Cross-scenario leak: `AppliedSpendingEvidence` lives on `WhatIfSettings`,
   which is per-scenario-file; `handleCreateScenario` builds a fresh
   settings struct rather than copying an existing one (grep found no
   scenario-duplication/clone-settings feature). Structurally cannot leak
   across scenarios — not reachable, no probe needed.

5. Seed-exactness / vacuous-test check for criterion (c). Read
   TestAppliedSpendingGraphByteIdenticalToSharedCore: it compares the live
   HTTP handler's body to a DIRECT call of `buildSpendingGraphPayload`
   using the SAME persisted `got` settings and the SAME evidence-derived
   inputs (not independently reconstructed/wrong inputs) — not vacuous.
   Manifest's mutation-kill (hardcoding seeds 424242 in the handler) broke
   this test, confirming it actually pins the seed plumbing. Verified the
   shared-core refactor itself: `buildSpendingGraphPayload` is the one
   function both `/whatif/spending/optimize/graph` and
   `/whatif/spending/applied/graph` call; no second graph-construction path
   exists in the diff.

6. Panel relocation / duplicate IDs. `#spending-graph-preview` is now
   defined once (`{{define "whatif-spending-graph-panel"}}` in
   spending_optimizer_results.html) and invoked once, outside the swapped
   `#spending-optimizer-results` div, in the persistent shell
   (spending_optimizer.html). Grepped the whole template tree: no second
   `id="spending-graph-preview"` or `id="spending-applied-plan"`. JS
   `form.addEventListener('submit', ...)` calls `invalidate()` →
   `purgeGraph()` (hides panel, clears heading/summary/note/data, resets
   `aria-pressed` on every `[data-spending-graph]` button including the
   applied one) BEFORE `results.innerHTML = markup` — a new comparison run
   cannot leave a stale chart visible, and the applied button's
   aria-pressed/panel state stays coherent whichever direction the user
   goes (applied → live preview, or live preview → applied).

7. Boost deep-clone. `cloneSpendingCandidate` clones `LivingSpendingBoost`
   and `Guardrails` then round-trips the WHOLE candidate through
   `json.Marshal`/`Unmarshal` — a genuine deep, independent clone including
   `Metrics`/`SimulationYears`/`WorstPathYears`. `evidenceRequest.LivingSpendingBoost`
   is explicitly `models.CloneLivingSpendingBoost`'d before being stored.
   Read-side `appliedSpendingEvidenceFresh` only shallow-copies
   `WhatIfSettings` to nil one field for hashing — read-only, no mutation
   risk.

8. Stale-chart honesty. On a fresh `GET /whatif`, the panel is `hidden`
   with all text nodes empty (server always renders it that way); the
   "Selected spending plan" block for stale evidence renders ONLY the note,
   with the button (`{{if $form.AppliedEvidenceFresh}}`) never emitted —
   confirmed in spending_optimizer.html. No path renders a chart for stale
   evidence: `handleAppliedSpendingGraphWithRunner` checks
   `appliedSpendingEvidenceFresh` before building anything and returns 409
   with a message containing "changed" (asserted by the shipped test and
   my own SpendingSearch-edit probe).

9. `spendingOptimizerFormData` sets all four `AppliedEvidence*` keys
   unconditionally before any branching (including for chained scenarios),
   so there is no code path that leaves them as a missing map key
   (`<no value>`) — confirmed by reading the function body directly.

10. Formatter discipline: `formatDate` is the single existing template
    helper (shared with the rest of the app, not a new date formatter);
    the only money figure in the new block goes through
    `spendingGraphCandidateLabel` → `budgettemplates.FormatMoney`
    (pre-existing, unchanged, reused).

11. Regression check on SP1/apply/preview/cancel: the two touched existing
    tests (TestSpendingApplyAtomicCompleteCandidate,
    TestSpendingApplySavesSearchPreferences) were extended, not weakened —
    they still assert the FULL settings byte-for-byte match after
    resetting the new field back, and were forced to change only because
    they assert "nothing else changed" and a 5th field is now legitimately
    written; verified this is the necessary/correct update, not a
    scope-widening or a hidden weakening, by tracing that an unmodified
    version of these tests would have failed on a CORRECT implementation
    (proving they had no choice). Confirmed `s.AppliedSpendingEvidence`
    and SP1's fields are written in exactly one `SaveWithRevisionIfScenario`
    call (single revision increment).

12. No critical-glob files touched (checked
    .swarm/critical.globs from the run dir against the manifest's file
    list — no overlap).

No FAIL found. The strongest candidate attack was #1 (hash sensitivity to
SP1's own SpendingSearch field, since the shipped test only exercised
DiscountRate) — it did NOT succeed; the guard is a whole-settings hash and
is sensitive to that field too, confirmed by a live probe against the
unmodified code.
